<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Holy Trinity" cic="CCC 232-267" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Section 1 &mdash; Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does it mean that the Trinity is the "central mystery of the Christian faith"? Why is it not just an advanced topic for theologians, but the foundation of everything else we believe?</li>
<li>What are the three divine Persons, and what is the eternal relationship between them? How is the Father related to the Son, and how is the Holy Spirit related to both?</li>
<li>What does "God is Love" (1 Jn 4:8, 16) mean for understanding the Trinity? Why is this sentence the most important key to the entire doctrine?</li>
<li>In the baptism of Jesus (Mt 3:16-17), how do the three Persons manifest themselves simultaneously? What does this visible epiphany of the Trinity teach us about who God is?</li>
<li>What is the difference between the Persons being "really distinct" and their sharing "one divine nature"? Why is it heresy to say either that there are three gods, or that God is one Person with three masks?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Section 2 &mdash; Deepening Your Understanding</h3>

<h4>2.1 The Progressive Revelation of the Trinity</h4>
<p>Explain in your own words why God did not reveal himself as Trinity all at once. What "Trinitarian glimpses" can be found in the Old Testament? How does the full revelation in Jesus Christ fulfill and surpass everything that came before?</p>
