<h3>Section 3 &mdash; For Reflection and Personal Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no "correct" answers to these personal questions — only thoughtful ones.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. If God is a communion of love from all eternity — a family of Persons who give themselves to one another — what does that tell us about the deepest meaning of our human relationships?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think about your family, your friendships, your community. Do you tend to live these relationships as communion and mutual self-gift, or as independence and self-protection? What would it look like to model your closest relationships more deliberately on the Trinitarian pattern?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Is it harder for you to imagine God as solitude or as communion? Which image of God feels closer to your actual experience in prayer?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Most people instinctively imagine God as a solitary being sitting on a throne. The Christian faith says He has never been alone: He has always been Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. Does this Trinitarian image of God change the way you would approach him in prayer? Does it make God feel more distant or closer to you?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
